Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero OS monolithic kernel - shared x86/x64 contract

All kernel code is in kernel.zero. There is no assembly driver, filesystem,
terminal implementation or borrowed runtime. Compiler intrinsics provide
individual memory/port operations, while all device protocol and command
logic is written explicitly in Zero.

Entry contract
The same kernel.zero compiles to 32-bit protected mode and 64-bit long mode.
Both enter at privilege level 0 with maskable interrupts disabled and a
loader-owned stack pool. Raw64 requires valid mappings; raw32 requires flat
code/data segments and may run without paging. Its full u64 value semantics
do not require PAE or long mode. The x64 entry receives the handoff in RCX;
IA32 receives it as cdecl arg0 at [ESP+4]. Source reads it through :arg0[].

The shared 256-byte handoff consists of u64 fields, with display metadata at
+48, boot kind at +96, execution architecture at +104, and owned stack-pool
base/size at +112/+120, native tool bundle base/size at +128/+136, and
compiler workspace base/size at +144/+152. BIOS supplies a validated VBE framebuffer or VGA text;
either UEFI loader supplies a validated GOP framebuffer when available.
UEFI allocates1MiB for the stack pool; BIOS verifies usable E820 RAM covering
0x100000..0x1000000, using1..4MiB as its3MiB kernel pool,4..5MiB for
the compiler bundle and8..16MiB for its8MiB workspace. UEFI allocates these
regions independently; see portability/guest/BUNDLE.ztext.
See BIOS.ztext, UEFI32.ztext and UEFI-CONSOLE.ztext. The kernel reserves
64KiB from the compiler arena. The first24KiB hold disk snapshots, line I/O
and console state; the remaining40KiB hold the common filesystem workspace
and two16KiB buffers. Source uses the data-first grammar in SYNTAX.ztext.

Terminal
The normal desktop has independent terminal windows managed by wm/*.zero.part.
Tiled pages and a horizontally scrolling strip share the original glyph
renderer. Exact1920x1080 uses2x interface scaling and16x32 physical cells;
other modes use1x. Output is buffered per window and reflowed to its bounds.
VGA fallback shows the focused session in80x24 cells below a title row;
serial output remains available. The kernel owns rendering and PS/2 input
and calls no BIOS or UEFI service after entry. See WINDOWS.ztext.
The keyboard uses a US ASCII layout, with Shift, Caps Lock, Enter and
Backspace. Tab inserts a space. It is not a USB keyboard driver.
COM1 UART at 115200 baud, 8N1 is optional for mirrored output and serial input.
Use an external serial terminal or QEMU serial stdio when desired. Missing or
stalled serial hardware disables serial I/O without stopping the visible
terminal. If no supported display is supplied, serial remains the output
fallback. See CONSOLE.ztext for detailed console and keyboard limits.
All characters are ASCII. CR and LF are accepted as serial line endings;
CRLF is treated as one Enter. Backspace and serial DEL erase one character.
Input limit: 480 bytes. If exceeded, the entire command is rejected even if
characters are subsequently erased. There is no command recall or mouse
input. Output history is a7168-byte ring per session.

Cooperative scheduling
There are three native kernel threads, displayed as1 terminal,2 graphics
and3 computation. Internal scheduler slots remain0..2.
Each has its own native stack, local frame and saved continuation. fork copies
the initial frame/live operands and preserves pointers to explicitly shared
buffers. The two child stacks occupy 81920-byte regions at the lower end of
the loader's owned pool; the original frame/arena remains at the top.
If the handoff does not provide a valid pool, worker creation is skipped and
tasks reports that failure while the terminal remains available.

The terminal performs a cooperative round-robin pass at its yield point,
switching to each runnable worker. The graphics worker yields during drawing;
the computation worker executes a block of 1024 linear-congruential steps,
publishes its u64 checksum and yields. This worker polls the network even
when computation is paused, so pause3 does not disable DHCP or HTTP. The
terminal thread stays runnable.
This is one CPU, one shared kernel address space and no timer preemption.
A worker that never yields would prevent the other threads from advancing.
Disk operations and output-ring writes remain in the terminal thread; no
filesystem locking or simultaneous ATA requests are introduced. Sessions
share this engine and do not become isolated processes. Focus changes only
at idle shell state10. Windows use8KiB slots in the owned stack-pool middle;
see wm/SESSION.ztext for exact ownership boundaries.

PIT channel 0 is polled with its IRQ masked to pace animation. Tick values
and worker counters are observability data, not a wall clock or scheduling
deadline guarantee. Device/output work can delay yields and animation.

Commands
search [ROOT|]PATTERN   list matching names; * matches any text, ? one character
del NAME              remove a file; rm remains an alias
net                   show automatic wired DHCP state and IP configuration
fetch HOST[:PORT][/PATH] bounded plain HTTP request, with DNS for hostnames
wifi                  open the keyboard-operated WiFi status/selection UI
apps                  open the keyboard application menu
system                live thread monitor; Enter toggles computation
storage               choose a volume root with arrows and Enter
network               live wired status and addresses; Enter opens WiFi
help                  list available commands
info                  describe the system and identify 32-bit/64-bit execution
clear                 clear only the focused terminal; ANSI clear on COM1
windows               list live terminal IDs, roots, focus and capacity
window ACTION|ID      create/close/focus or select tile/scroll; see WINDOWS.ztext
zero SOURCE           compile and run a short Zero program
ezero SOURCE          compile and run a short Ezero program
net                   show wired connection and DHCP addresses
wifi                  open the keyboard wireless setup interface
fetch HOST[:PORT]/PATH fetch bounded ASCII HTTP/1.0 content
write TEXT            print text
disks                 list independent volumes, sizes and formats
GO B:|                select a volume root; lowercase go also works
watch [B:|]           list names in the current or explicitly selected root
create NAME           create an empty file; an existing file is preserved
save NAME TEXT        create or replace a file; empty TEXT empties the file
read NAME             read a file
rm NAME               delete a file
format ZERO           erase the selected dedicated raw primary ZeroFS disk
tasks                 show thread states, progress, checksum and sampled ticks
pause 2|3             pause graphics (2) or computation (3)
resume 2|3            resume the selected worker
animate               toggle focused terminal caret blink
theme                 switch between the desktop color themes
pixel X Y R G B       write one framebuffer pixel; decimal RGB values 0..255
reset                 reset the computation counter and request a new sequence

Graphics commands need a supported linear framebuffer. Coordinates must lie
inside it; coordinates refer to physical pixels, independent of UI scale.
A manually written pixel can be overwritten by later drawing;
pause 2 freezes the entire display while input/commands continue and their
output is buffered. resume 2 displays the latest contents. Changes
requested by animate/theme appear when that worker runs again.
These display and scheduler settings are volatile and do not change files.
reset is a background-computation command, not reboot or disk formatting.

Names are case-sensitive, 1..31 characters from A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -.
Commands without operands reject extra arguments. format requires the exact
argument ZERO; incomplete or additional arguments never initialize the disk.
ZeroFS has8 files with up to400 ASCII bytes of text each. Foreign formats have
their own bounded capacities, documented in fs/*-profile.txt. Paths may be
relative names or absolute A:|NAME. Dot and dot-dot are rejected. Directories, rename,
append, binary files and permissions are not implemented in this version.
After the one name/text separating space, additional spaces are preserved.

Storage
Four primary/secondary master/slave ATA PIO positions are scanned. The boot
medium remains separate. Read-only IDENTIFY validation requires an ATA disk
with LBA28, at least 18 sectors, 512-byte logical sectors and valid FLUSH CACHE
support before any filesystem-sector access. Capacity is capped at the
LBA28 address limit. Commands wait for both BSY and
DRQ to clear before the next operation and after a data transfer.
A blank data.img is deliberately not autoformatted.
The user must issue format ZERO to initialize it. That command erases both
snapshots, so it is not a recovery operation. Never point this experimental
filesystem at an unrelated disk containing data to preserve. This formatter
is unavailable for foreign filesystems or partitioned volumes. See DRIVES.ztext
for discovery, independent roots and filesystem dispatch. Each foreign driver
reloads and validates metadata for every operation, including after changing
volumes. The common disk layer checks physical capacity, request-buffer bounds
and the selected volume bounds independently of each driver's checks.

ZeroFS disk layout (512-byte sectors)
The layout and full u64 generation/checksum arithmetic are identical in both
kernel builds; switching CPU or firmware mode does not convert the data disk.
Bank A: LBA 0 header, LBA 1..8 data.
Bank B: LBA 9 header, LBA 10..17 data.
Each record is one 512-byte sector:
byte 0: used flag (0/1)
bytes 1..32: NUL-terminated name
bytes 34..35: little-endian content length (0..400)
bytes 36 onward: ASCII text followed by NUL, remaining bytes zeroed.
Header byte 0: 64-bit magic 0x31303053464f525a (ZROFS001)
Header byte 8: nonzero 64-bit generation
Header byte 16: weighted byte checksum of all 4096 data bytes
Header byte 24: magic + generation + data checksum modulo 2^64
Data checksum: sum(byte_at_bank_offset_i * (i + 1)), i=512..4607.

Commit sequence: inactive-bank data, ATA FLUSH CACHE, inactive-bank header,
ATA FLUSH CACHE. Only then is Saved to disk reported. On error, file access
is disabled until restart because final commit outcome may be uncertain.
Mount selects the highest-generation structurally valid checksummed copy.
Duplicate file names invalidate a snapshot even if its checksum is valid.
Generation wrap is refused. Unknown or doubly corrupt disks stay unavailable.
The checksum detects ordinary damage; it is neither encryption nor protection
against intentional forgery. Removing a file is not secure erasure.

Kernel limitations
The kernel polls devices and schedules cooperatively. There is no preemption,
SMP, process isolation, IDT/exception recovery, arbitrary GUI applications,
physical WiFi connection drivers, TLS, USB/SATA/AHCI drivers or dynamic
partition resizing. Native Zero/Ezero apps use bounded memory streams but
share kernel memory; unsafe stores or an infinite loop can damage or stall
the system. Automatic PCnet/DHCP, DNS and bounded TCP/HTTP are implemented in
net/*.zero.part. The modal WiFi UI reports unsupported hardware honestly;
its test fixtures do not establish a physical radio connection.
CPU usage while waiting for terminal input is expected.
ATA, PS/2 setup and serial-transmit waits are bounded by iteration counts,
not a calibrated physical timer. Serial-transmit failure disables COM1;
visible output continues. Keyboard input requires a supported PS/2 controller.

Historical 0.4.0 verification
The final 0.4.0 ISO booted BIOS32 without LM/PAE, BIOS64, IA32 OVMF and AMD64
OVMF. Visible-console/PS/2 checks passed, and one shared disk retained file
contents through full process restarts across 32/64-bit firmware paths.
Actual VirtualBox BIOS firmware under QEMU also booted the 32-bit path and
preserved files. Native VirtualBox execution remains unverified because its
host driver is unavailable. See verification.ztext for exact artifacts, logs,
test scope and remaining limits.

The pixel command uses one-based physical coordinates: X1..width and
Y1..height. RGB values remain0..255. Internal framebuffer offsets remain
zero-based; this user-facing change begins in0.9.0.

Keyboard applications0.10
The45x12 pseudo-graphic frame reuses the original renderer and focused-window
modal ownership. console2472 is application kind(0 WiFi,1 menu,2 system,
3 storage,4 network),2480 is one-based selection,2488 the last refresh tick.
apps/*.zero.part provides behavior and labels; net/kernel-integrate.sh expands
only whitelisted NET then APP includes. Application refresh does not append to
the saved terminal ring. Esc restores the session; Backspace returns to menu.
Storage selection changes only the selected volume index, never disk sectors.
System Enter toggles computation state1/2; networking continues. The new host
file primitives return explicit unavailable behavior in guest apps. host()=0;
Ezero arguments/readfile/writefile/fileexists report a source-line error here.
